WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.477
COALESCE provides fallback logic.

00:00:02.477 --> 00:00:09.292
PostgreSQL evaluates the expressions from left to right and returns the first one that is not NULL.

00:00:09.292 --> 00:00:12.552
A common example is contact information.

00:00:12.552 --> 00:00:16.892
We may prefer email first, then mobile phone, then home phone,

00:00:16.892 --> 00:00:21.347
and finally a text label such as No contact provided.

00:00:21.347 --> 00:00:26.507
But there is an important detail: COALESCE only understands NULL.

00:00:26.507 --> 00:00:31.897
It does not know that an empty string or a few spaces are useless contact information.

00:00:31.897 --> 00:00:38.062
If email contains a blank string, COALESCE may stop there and return that blank value instead of moving

00:00:38.062 --> 00:00:45.892
to the mobile number. That is why COALESCE is often paired with normalization using BTRIM and NULLIF.

00:00:45.892 --> 00:00:52.702
COALESCE also appears in numeric work, but you need to be careful with meaning.

00:00:52.702 --> 00:00:57.647
Replacing a missing amount with zero is not just a technical operation.

00:00:57.647 --> 00:01:00.147
It makes a business assumption that missing means none.

00:01:00.147 --> 00:01:06.467
For example, if amount_paid is NULL because no payment was made, zero may be reasonable.

00:01:06.467 --> 00:01:12.357
But if the amount is NULL because the payment system failed to load data,

00:01:12.357 --> 00:01:15.277
replacing it with zero could be misleading.

00:01:15.277 --> 00:01:20.627
So the rule is not “use COALESCE whenever you see NULL.” The rule is “use COALESCE when the

00:01:20.627 --> 00:01:24.699
fallback value has a clear, documented meaning.”
